ase in Southern log sales attributable to a 1 percent increase in sales volumes, as well as a $9 million increase in stumpage and pay-as-cut timber sales attributable to increased sales realizations and sales volumes.</span></p><p style="font-size:10pt;margin-top:6pt;font-family:Times New Roman;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left;"><span style="color:#39754e;white-space:pre-wrap;font-weight:bold;font-size:8pt;font-family:Arial;font-kerning:none;min-width:fit-content;">Intersegment Sales</span></p><p style="font-size:10pt;margin-top:6pt;font-family:Times New Roman;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left;"><span style="color:#000000;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:8pt;font-family:Arial;font-kerning:none;min-width:fit-content;">Intersegment sales increased $38 million &#8212; 7 percent &#8212; primarily due to a 3 percent increase in sales realizations, as well as a 3 percent increase in sales volumes.</span></p><p style="font-size:10pt;margin-top:6pt;font-family:Times New Roman;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left;"><span style="color:#39754e;white-space:pre-wrap;font-weight:bold;font-size:8pt;font-family:Arial;font-kerning:none;min-width:fit-content;">Costs of Sales</span></p><p style="font-size:10pt;margin-top:6pt;font-family:Times New Roman;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left;"><span style="color:#000000;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:8pt;font-family:Arial;font-kerning:none;min-width:fit-content;">Costs of sales decreased $22 million &#8212; 1 percent &#8212; primarily due to decreased Western sales volumes, partially offset by increased Southern sales volumes.</span></p><p style="font-size:10pt;margin-top:6pt;font-family:Times New Roman;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left;"><span style="color:#39754e;white-space:pre-wrap;font-weight:bold;font-size:8pt;font-family:Arial;font-kerning:none;min-width:fit-content;">Net Contribution to Earnings</span></p><p style="font-size:10pt;margin-top:6pt;font-family:Times New Roman;margin-bottom:6pt;text-align:left;"><span style="color:#000000;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:8pt;font-family:Arial;font-kerning:none;min-width:fit-content;">Net contribution to earnings increased $306 million &#8212; 109 percent &#8212; primarily due to a $266 million increase in gain on sale of timberlands (refer to </span><a href="#fn_timberland_acquisitions_divestitures"><span style="color:#0000ff;white-space:pre-wrap;text-decoration:underline solid;font-size:8pt;font-family:Arial;font-style:italic;font-kerning:none;min-width:fit-content;">Note 4: Timberland Acquisitions and Divestitures</span></a><span style="color:#000000;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:8pt;font-family:Arial;font-kerning:none;min-width:fit-content;">), as well as the change in the components of gross margin, as discussed above.</span><span style="color:#000000;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:8pt;font-family:Arial;font-kerning:none;min-width:fit-content;"> </span></p></div>
